### 4\. Update `Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml`

We need three changes:

* Reference the locally installed Bootstrap Icons stylesheet.

* Initialize the saved theme in the `<head>` before the stylesheets load.

* Replace the old text-and-icon button with an icon-only button.

### Step 1 — Add the Bootstrap Icons stylesheet

Find the `<head>` section.

Place this reference after your Bootstrap CSS and before the closing `</head>`:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet"
      href="~/lib/bootstrap-icons/font/bootstrap-icons.min.css"
      asp-append-version="true" />
```

The location is important because the CSS file is inside:

```text
wwwroot/lib/bootstrap-icons/font/
```

The font references inside the Bootstrap Icons stylesheet are relative to its own location. Therefore, keeping the `fonts` folder alongside the CSS file's expected relative path is essential.

### Step 2 — Add the early theme initialization

Place this script immediately after your `<meta>` tags and before your CSS references.

```html
<script>
    (() => {
        const root = document.documentElement;

        try {
            const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem("theme");

            const isValidTheme =
                savedTheme === "light" ||
                savedTheme === "dark";

            const systemPrefersDark =
                window.matchMedia &&
                window.matchMedia(
                    "(prefers-color-scheme: dark)"
                ).matches;

            const theme = isValidTheme
                ? savedTheme
                : (systemPrefersDark ? "dark" : "light");

            root.setAttribute("data-theme", theme);
            root.setAttribute("data-bs-theme", theme);

        } catch (error) {
            root.setAttribute("data-theme", "light");
            root.setAttribute("data-bs-theme", "light");
        }
    })();
</script>
```

Why do we put this here?

The browser processes the HTML from top to bottom.

If the saved theme is applied only by JavaScript at the bottom of the page, the browser might initially display Light Mode before switching to Dark Mode.

By initializing the theme before the stylesheets, the browser can use the correct CSS variables during the initial rendering.

### Step 3 — Replace the existing theme button

Find your existing theme toggle button in the navbar.

Remove the old button, including its visible text and any inline SVG.

Replace it with:

```html

<button type="button"
        id="themeToggle"
        class="btn btn-outline-secondary theme-toggle-btn"
        aria-label="Switch to Dark Mode"
        title="Switch to Dark Mode"
        aria-pressed="false">

    <i id="themeIcon"
       class="bi bi-moon-fill"
       aria-hidden="true"></i>

</button>
```

Place this button inside your existing navbar, preferably near the right side.

For example:

```html
<div class="d-flex align-items-center ms-auto">
    <!-- Theme toggle button goes here -->
</div>
```

### Understanding the button

| Attribute          | Purpose                                                      |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| type="button"      | Prevents accidental form submission                          |
| id="themeToggle"   | Allows JavaScript to identify the button                     |
| theme-toggle-btn   | Applies our custom compact styling                           |
| aria-label         | Provides an accessible description                           |
| title              | Displays a tooltip on hover                                  |
| aria-pressed       | Announces the current toggle state                           |
| id="themeIcon"     | Allows JavaScript to update the icon                         |
| aria-hidden="true" | Prevents screen readers from redundantly announcing the icon |

The icon class:

```html
class="bi bi-moon-fill"
```

displays the filled moon.

For Dark Mode, JavaScript will change it to:

```html
class="bi bi-sun-fill"
```

No inline SVG, CDN, or Font Awesome is needed.

### 5\. Update `wwwroot/js/theme.js`

Now we will replace your existing theme JavaScript with a cleaner implementation.

This version handles all the requirements, including mouse/touch blur and keyboard accessibility.

### Complete updated JavaScript

Open `wwwroot/js/theme.js`, remove the old implementation, and paste:

```javascript

"use strict";

(() => {

    // =========================================
    // 1. CONFIGURATION
    // =========================================

    const STORAGE_KEY = "theme";

    const root = document.documentElement;

    // =========================================
    // 2. VALIDATE THEME VALUE
    // =========================================

    function isValidTheme(theme) {

        return theme === "light" || theme === "dark";

    }

    // =========================================
    // 3. GET SAVED THEME
    // =========================================

    function getSavedTheme() {

        try {

            const savedTheme =
                localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);

            return isValidTheme(savedTheme)
                ? savedTheme
                : null;

        } catch (error) {

            console.warn(
                "Unable to read theme from Local Storage.",
                error
            );

            return null;

        }

    }

    // =========================================
    // 4. APPLY THEME
    // =========================================

    function applyTheme(theme) {

        if (!isValidTheme(theme)) {

            theme = "light";

        }

        root.setAttribute("data-theme", theme);

        // Synchronize Bootstrap 5.3 color mode.
        root.setAttribute("data-bs-theme", theme);

        return theme;

    }

    // =========================================
    // 5. SAVE THEME
    // =========================================

    function saveTheme(theme) {

        try {

            localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, theme);

            return true;

        } catch (error) {

            console.warn(
                "Unable to save theme to Local Storage.",
                error
            );

            return false;

        }

    }

    // =========================================
    // 6. UPDATE ICON AND ACCESSIBILITY
    // =========================================

    function updateThemeIcon(theme, button, icon) {

        if (!button || !icon) {

            return;

        }

        const isDark = theme === "dark";

        // Light Mode = Moon
        // Dark Mode = Sun

        icon.classList.toggle("bi-moon-fill", !isDark);

        icon.classList.toggle("bi-sun-fill", isDark);

        // Update accessible button label.

        const actionText = isDark
            ? "Switch to Light Mode"
            : "Switch to Dark Mode";

        button.setAttribute("aria-label", actionText);

        button.setAttribute("title", actionText);

        // Announce current toggle state.

        button.setAttribute(
            "aria-pressed",
            String(isDark)
        );

    }

    // =========================================
    // 7. INITIALIZE THEME
    // =========================================

    function initializeTheme() {

        const button =
            document.getElementById("themeToggle");

        const icon =
            document.getElementById("themeIcon");

        // Stop safely if the button or icon is missing.

        if (!button || !icon) {

            return;

        }

        // Prevent duplicate event listeners.

        if (button.dataset.themeInitialized === "true") {

            return;

        }

        button.dataset.themeInitialized = "true";

        // Read the saved preference.

        const savedTheme = getSavedTheme();

        // Use the theme already applied by the head script
        // if there is no saved preference.

        const currentTheme =
            root.getAttribute("data-theme");

        const initialTheme = savedTheme ||
            (isValidTheme(currentTheme)
                ? currentTheme
                : "light");

        // Apply the initial theme.

        const theme = applyTheme(initialTheme);

        // Update the icon and accessible attributes.

        updateThemeIcon(theme, button, icon);

        // =========================================
        // 8. THEME TOGGLE EVENT
        // =========================================

        button.addEventListener("click", function (event) {

            // Read current theme.

            const current =
                root.getAttribute("data-theme");

            // Determine opposite theme.

            const newTheme = current === "dark"
                ? "light"
                : "dark";

            // Apply the new theme.

            const appliedTheme = applyTheme(newTheme);

            // Save the selection.

            saveTheme(appliedTheme);

            // Update icon and accessibility attributes.

            updateThemeIcon(
                appliedTheme,
                button,
                icon
            );

            // Remove visual focus after mouse or touch
            // activation, but preserve keyboard focus.

            if (event.detail > 0) {

                button.blur();

            }

        });

    }

    // =========================================
    // 9. DOM INITIALIZATION
    // =========================================

    if (document.readyState === "loading") {

        document.addEventListener(
            "DOMContentLoaded",
            initializeTheme,
            { once: true }
        );

    } else {

        initializeTheme();

    }

})();
```

### Explanation of the important JavaScript sections

1\. Theme validation

```javascript
function isValidTheme(theme) {
    return theme === "light" || theme === "dark";
}
```

This prevents unexpected values from being applied.

For example, if someone manually changes Local Storage to:

```text
theme = blue
```

the application will not accept that value as a valid theme.

2\. Retrieve the saved theme

```javascript
localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
```

The application reads the same `theme` key used by your previous implementation. Existing valid preferences are therefore retained.

3\. Apply the theme

```javascript
root.setAttribute("data-theme", theme);
root.setAttribute("data-bs-theme", theme);
```

The first attribute controls your custom CSS. The second synchronizes Bootstrap 5.3 color modes.

4\. Update the icon

```javascript
icon.classList.toggle("bi-moon-fill", !isDark);
icon.classList.toggle("bi-sun-fill", isDark);
```

This changes the Bootstrap Icon class dynamically.

When Light Mode is active:

```html
<i class="bi bi-moon-fill"></i>
```

When Dark Mode is active:

```html
<i class="bi bi-sun-fill"></i>
```

Notice that we do not need two separate icon elements. One element is sufficient.

5\. Update accessibility attributes

```javascript
button.setAttribute("aria-label", actionText);
button.setAttribute("title", actionText);
```

The user will see only the icon, but screen readers and other assistive technologies can identify what the button does.

6\. Prevent duplicate event listeners

```javascript
if (button.dataset.themeInitialized === "true") {
    return;
}
```

This ensures that the same button is not initialized repeatedly.

7\. Preserve keyboard accessibility

The important part is:

```javascript
if (event.detail > 0) {
    button.blur();
}
```

Here is how it works:

| Interaction | event.detail           | Result                    |
| ----------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Mouse click | Usually greater than 0 | Button loses visual focus |
| Touch tap   | Usually greater than 0 | Button loses visual focus |
| Enter key   | 0                      | Keyboard focus remains    |
| Space key   | 0                      | Keyboard focus remains    |

`blur()` removes focus from the button. We only call it for pointer-generated clicks, not keyboard-generated clicks.

This distinction is important because keyboard users need to know which element currently has focus.

8\. Safe initialization

```javascript
if (document.readyState === "loading")
```

If the HTML is still loading, JavaScript waits for `DOMContentLoaded`. Otherwise, it initializes immediately.

This avoids errors caused by trying to find the button before the browser has parsed it.

### 6\. Update `wwwroot/css/theme.css`

We will add a compact icon-only button and improve its focus behavior.

You do not need to replace your existing theme variables, card styles, table styles, or other CSS.

Add this section at the bottom of your existing `theme.css`.

```css

/* =========================================
   THEME TOGGLE BUTTON
   ========================================= */

.theme-toggle-btn {

    width: 38px;
    height: 38px;

    padding: 0;

    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;

    border-radius: 50%;

    font-size: 1.05rem;

    line-height: 1;

    flex-shrink: 0;

    transition:
        background-color 0.2s ease,
        color 0.2s ease,
        border-color 0.2s ease,
        transform 0.15s ease;

}

/* Icon alignment */

.theme-toggle-btn i {

    display: inline-block;

    line-height: 1;

}

/* Mouse hover */

.theme-toggle-btn:hover {

    transform: translateY(-1px);

}

/* Active press */

.theme-toggle-btn:active {

    transform: scale(0.96);

}

/* Keyboard focus indicator */

.theme-toggle-btn:focus-visible {

    outline: 3px solid var(--app-primary);

    outline-offset: 3px;

    box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--app-background);

}

/* Do not show a custom outline for
   ordinary pointer focus. */

.theme-toggle-btn:focus:not(:focus-visible) {

    outline: none;

    box-shadow: none;

}

/* Respect reduced-motion settings */

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {

    .theme-toggle-btn {

        transition: none;

    }

}
```

### Explanation of the CSS

| CSS                        | Purpose                                        |
| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| width: 38px                | Makes the button compact                       |
| height: 38px               | Keeps the button square                        |
| padding: 0                 | Removes unnecessary internal spacing           |
| inline-flex                | Allows the button to fit naturally in a navbar |
| align-items: center        | Centers the icon vertically                    |
| justify-content: center    | Centers the icon horizontally                  |
| border-radius: 50%         | Makes the button circular                      |
| flex-shrink: 0             | Prevents the button from becoming too small    |
| :hover                     | Adds a subtle hover effect                     |
| :active                    | Adds a small press animation                   |
| :focus-visible             | Displays a visible keyboard focus indicator    |
| :focus:not(:focus-visible) | Avoids unnecessary pointer-focus styling       |

### Important accessibility note

We are not removing the keyboard focus indicator.

The following rule is intentional:

```css
.theme-toggle-btn:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--app-primary);
    outline-offset: 3px;
}
```

It makes the button visibly identifiable when someone navigates using Tab, Enter, or Space.

The pointer-focus rule only removes the ordinary outline when the browser does not consider the focus visible.

